===Etymology===
From a form of {{der|en|nl|gurk}}, an archaic variant of {{m|nl|augurk|t=small pickled cucumber}}, from {{der|en|nds|-}}, from {{der|en|gml|agurke}}, {{m|gml|augurke}}, probably via {{der|en|sla|-}} (compare {{cog|pl|ogórek}}), from {{der|en|gkm|ἀγγούριον||cucumber}}. The underlying Dutch form may be a diminutive ({{m|nl||gurkijn}}) or perhaps more probably the plural ({{m|nl||gurken}}), which was then associated with the English suffix {{m|en|-kin}} (itself incidentally from Dutch or Low German).<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|gherkin}}</ref><ref>{{R:nl:Etymologiebank|augurk}}</ref> Compare German {{m|de|Gurke}}.

